# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets. Never tracked. `.env.example` is the template that IS tracked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.local
*.pem
*.key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Patient data and everything derived from it.
#
# `outputs/` is a scratch directory: the cohort CSV, every sub-CSV the data
# stage cuts from it, the matcher's column catalogue (all 1,394 names), and one
# folder per run. None of it belongs in a repository, and all of it is
# regenerable — see prep_hiv_dataset.py, make_sample_data.py, prep_nhanes.py and
# scripts/build_matcher_cache.py.
#
# The two entries below are the exceptions: the README explaining the folder,
# and the .gitkeep that keeps the empty folder present after a clone.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
outputs/*
!outputs/.gitkeep
!outputs/README.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Knowledge-graph dumps. AlzKB and AddictionKB are published elsewhere; these
# are 1GB of exports and their SQLite builds. The scripts that produce and read
# them are tracked, the payloads are not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
kg/**/*.db
kg/**/*.cypherl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reinstallable
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv
.venv/
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The rule files, which the global ignore would otherwise swallow.
#

# the grounds that it is local-only assistant tooling. In this repository it is
# not. These five files are the rules themselves — they cite line numbers that
# `scripts/check_citations.py` verifies on every run, and a citation in an
# untracked file is never checked, because the checker indexes `git ls-files`.
# `CLAUDE_KR.md` is a Korean edition and is ignored by the `*_KR*` rule below,
# which is a separate decision from this one.
#
# ASAREE and motoro both track theirs, and are unaffected by the global rule
# only because a file already tracked stays tracked. This repository has to say
# so out loud. The negation is deliberately un-anchored, so it covers the three

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Korean editions. Local only, as of 2026-09-02.
#
# Every Korean file in this project carries `_KR` in its name — that is the

# judgement about content, precisely so a pattern like this one can enforce it.
# The pattern is `*_KR*`, unanchored and open at both ends, because the marker
# lands in three different places: before the suffix (`BACKLOG_KR.md`), in the
# middle of a compound name (`..._KR_expanded.tex`, `..._KR_fig1.png`), and
# inside a versioned name (`EcoXAI_SRS_KR_v0.2_원본.pdf`). Anchoring it to
# `*_KR.*` would have caught 102 of the 108 and quietly let 6 through.
#
# **What this costs, stated once so nobody rediscovers it as a surprise.** All
# 108 files stay on disk and leave the repository, so a clone does not have


# to them from the English files still read correctly and resolve to nothing on
# GitHub. `scripts/check_citations.py` reports citations into them as *not
# verifiable here* rather than broken, by name (`KR_EDITION`).
#
# `outputs/` is ignored because its contents must not be published;
# these are ignored because the user asked for a repository whose tracked
# surface is English. The mechanism is the same, the reason is not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*_KR*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The three Korean files that could not be renamed. Local only, as of 2026-09-02.
#
# The `*_KR*` rule above works by filename, and these three cannot take that
# name. They are listed by path instead, one line each, because the reason is
# different for every one of them and a pattern would hide that.
#

#       Builds the Korean slide deck. Its Korean is slide copy inside string
#       literals — being Korean is what the file is *for*. Renaming a `.py` to
#       `*_KR*` would gitignore an executable under a rule meant for prose.
#

#       Opens with the original memo, transcribed exactly as it was written.
#       Translating it would make it stop being the original.
#
#   scripts/backlog_to_issues.py
#       Two comment lines quote that memo verbatim (`FOLD_IDS`). **This is the
#       expensive one**: the repository now does not contain the script that

#       reproduce the board. It was excluded on instruction, and the cheap way
#       back is to rewrite those two lines as a citation rather than a quotation
#       and track the file again.
#


# tree, not the index. What is lost is that neither is checked any more —
# `scripts/check_citations.py` indexes `git ls-files`, so citations *inside*
# these three stop being verified, and citations *into* them are reported as
# not verifiable here, by name (`LOCAL_ONLY`).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


scripts/backlog_to_issues.py

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# macOS and Office leavings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
~$*
